# Network retry settings, added because `npm audit` is a MANDATORY deploy gate in infra/deploy.sh
# (see RUNBOOK.md and STANDARDS.md 4b: "a report-only security gate is not a gate") and it was failing
# for a reason that had nothing to do with vulnerabilities.
#
# MEASURED CAUSE (2026-09-04), not a guess:
#   TCP connect to registry.npmjs.org        -> 0.18s, fine
#   GET  /lodash                             -> HTTP 200, 247 KB, 0.35s
#   POST /lodash                             -> HTTP 405, 0.5s        (so POST is NOT blocked)
#   POST /-/npm/v1/security/advisories/bulk  -> succeeds ~1 attempt in 5, otherwise the request is
#                                               accepted and then never answered (read timeout)
#   POST /-/npm/v1/security/audits/quick     -> same
# There is no IPv6 record, so that common cause is ruled out. Only the two security-advisory paths are
# affected, and only intermittently — something on the path (rate limiting or a middlebox) drops most
# POSTs to them.
#
# Consequence without these settings: `npm audit` fails with the opaque message
# "audit endpoint returned an error", deploy.sh aborts at step 1/5, and the natural next move for
# whoever is deploying is to delete the audit line. That would trade a real security control for a
# network flake. Retrying is the correct fix: the gate still has to return a genuine
# "no high-severity vulnerabilities" result, it just gets enough attempts to obtain one.
#
# 8 retries at ~20% success per attempt leaves roughly a 1-in-6 chance of the whole command still
# failing. If it does, re-run it — do NOT remove the gate. If it becomes a persistent blocker, the
# durable fixes are to allow those two paths through the network egress filter, or to deploy from CI.
fetch-retries=15
fetch-retry-mintimeout=2000
fetch-retry-maxtimeout=20000
fetch-timeout=60000
